1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,800
So if you have ever stood at a government counter, you know, filling out your name, your address, and your ID number on a clipboard for like the fifth time that year, you already know the system is just totally broken.

2
00:00:10,940 --> 00:00:12,760
Oh, absolutely. It's incredibly frustrating.

3
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:18,720
Right. But today we are unpacking exactly why you are stuck doing that.

4
00:00:19,220 --> 00:00:24,440
Welcome to this deep dive where we are looking at the hidden mechanics of government digital systems.

5
00:00:24,440 --> 00:00:33,300
We're using some pretty dense guidelines today, like the PRA framework and the EU-European Interoperability Framework.

6
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:35,340
Which are basically massive rescue manuals, right?

7
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:39,820
Yeah, exactly. They are designed to teach governments how to make their computers actually talk to each other.

8
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:44,040
Right. Because, I mean, we tend to view the government as the single cohesive entity.

9
00:00:44,040 --> 00:00:48,880
You think, OK, I gave my info to the tax office, so the health department should obviously have it.

10
00:00:48,940 --> 00:00:52,540
But under the hood, the digital infrastructure is completely fractured.

11
00:00:52,540 --> 00:00:58,960
It's like a city where every single neighborhood hires a totally different contractor to build its plumbing.

12
00:00:59,320 --> 00:00:59,600
Yeah.

13
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:02,600
And they use completely incompatible pipes.

14
00:01:03,020 --> 00:01:05,960
And then everyone stands around wondering why the water won't flow across town.

15
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:07,100
That is exactly it.

16
00:01:07,220 --> 00:01:12,160
And the ultimate casualty of those incompatible pipes is what they call the ask once principle.

17
00:01:12,480 --> 00:01:16,820
Oh, right. The idea that you give your ID to the government one time and that is it.

18
00:01:16,820 --> 00:01:26,640
Yeah. Well, that is the dream. But instead, you show your ID at the clinic and then you still have to bring a paper photocopy to enroll your kid in school.

19
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:33,040
And there are really four glaring signs from the sources that show a government lacks a shared plan to fix this.

20
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:34,180
OK. So what is the first one?

21
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:38,120
First is having multiple conflicting registers of the exact same people.

22
00:01:38,120 --> 00:01:45,620
So the school system builds their own list of citizens, the social registry builds theirs, and they just do not match.

23
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:47,660
Man, that sounds incredibly inefficient.

24
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:50,040
It is. And the second sign is what happens next.

25
00:01:50,480 --> 00:01:57,700
When the health ministry suddenly needs to verify a business owner, developers have to build a custom system-to-system connection from absolute scratch.

26
00:01:58,040 --> 00:02:00,180
Wait, really? Just for that one interaction?

27
00:02:00,180 --> 00:02:09,880
Yeah. Every single time. Which leads directly to the third sign, which is severe vendor lock-in, because nobody else knows how this specific vendor's code works.

28
00:02:09,940 --> 00:02:10,340
Oh, wow.

29
00:02:10,580 --> 00:02:18,440
Yeah. So a decade later, the government is essentially held hostage, you know, paying extortionate prices just to update a basic database.

30
00:02:18,980 --> 00:02:25,460
And the fourth sign is that ask once principle existing purely on paper while citizens do all the manual labor.

31
00:02:25,460 --> 00:02:35,560
I mean, I have to push back here a little bit, though. If this is so obviously broken, why do ministries keep building isolated silos? These agencies are run by smart people.

32
00:02:35,960 --> 00:02:43,720
It looks like total disorganization, I know. But here is where it gets really interesting. These systems are actually functioning exactly as they were designed to.

33
00:02:43,720 --> 00:02:45,240
They're designed to be disconnected.

34
00:02:45,560 --> 00:02:46,420
Functionally, yes.

35
00:02:46,620 --> 00:02:46,780
Yeah.

36
00:02:46,860 --> 00:02:47,980
Because of how they are funded.

37
00:02:47,980 --> 00:02:55,940
Like an outside donor, say the World Bank or some bilateral partner, will give a grant specifically for a new maternal health registry.

38
00:02:56,440 --> 00:02:59,900
So the health ministry hires a vendor and builds that exact registry.

39
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:02,920
Ah, I see. Their mandate is strictly just that one thing.

40
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:07,640
Right. They are not getting paid to integrate that new software with the national tax grid.

41
00:03:07,840 --> 00:03:11,600
They just execute their specific mandate in a total vacuum.

42
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:19,280
So because they are acting exactly as designed, they end up heavily taxing the country in, what, like four different directions at once?

43
00:03:19,360 --> 00:03:32,560
Exactly. You get wasted money on duplicate systems. You get lost time waiting for unplanned IT fixes. The citizens are obviously burdened. And then ministers literally cannot launch flagship cross-ministry programs.

44
00:03:32,760 --> 00:03:35,240
Because the underlying tech just refuses to communicate.

45
00:03:35,320 --> 00:03:35,780
Exactly.

46
00:03:35,780 --> 00:03:42,140
Wow. So what is the root cause of all this chaos? To me, this sounds like a wildly tangled server room.

47
00:03:42,280 --> 00:03:52,340
It is. And the root cause is not having a national enterprise architecture or an EA. There is basically no written record of what systems exist, who owns them, or how they fit together.

48
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:02,540
So an EA is basically a master blueprint. Because, you know, going back to the server room thing, you cannot just start unplugging things or buying new routers. You cannot fix the network until you actually map the wires.

49
00:04:02,540 --> 00:04:16,840
That is a perfect analogy. The EA is a hard-written law for that server room. And implementing this EA life cycle, you know, discovering, assessing, adapting, planning, it takes about six months to establish a solid roadmap.

50
00:04:17,100 --> 00:04:24,700
Oh, so totally changes the rules of engagement then. If a foreign donor wants to fund a new education app, they cannot just build it in a vacuum anymore.

51
00:04:24,700 --> 00:04:34,960
Exactly. With a national EA in place, the government can finally say, thank you for the funding. But to build this, your vendor must plug into our central data blueprint.

52
00:04:35,360 --> 00:04:42,280
That makes so much sense. It forces every stubborn, isolated ministry and their donors to operate from a single schematic.

53
00:04:42,520 --> 00:04:42,760
Right.

54
00:04:43,020 --> 00:04:54,000
So the next time you find yourself filling out your basic information for a government service for the fifth time, you will know exactly who to blame. The lack of a national enterprise architecture. The pipes were just built without a master plan.

55
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:57,080
Yeah. And that actually raises a fascinating question for you to consider.

56
00:04:57,280 --> 00:04:58,720
Ooh. What is that?

57
00:04:58,960 --> 00:05:10,100
If implementing a shared enterprise architecture blueprint is the secret weapon to forcing massive government ministries to finally communicate, look around at the other major institutions in your life.

58
00:05:10,700 --> 00:05:11,200
Like what?

59
00:05:11,500 --> 00:05:22,460
Well, what other massive, frustratingly siloed systems, you know, like major healthcare networks or university campuses, are secretly failing simply because nobody bothered to write down a master plan?

60
00:05:22,460 --> 00:05:30,760
That is a great point. Until somebody maps out the wires, we are all just going to be stuck filling out the same paperwork forever. Keep that in mind next time you ran into the pen.

